\section{Resolve a Package Graph Before Resolving Its Names}

A manifest is a set of requests: package names, version ranges,
source choices, and perhaps features or target conditions. A
lock graph is a particular solution. The Rust book's example
contrasts a Cargo manifest's permitted versions with the exact
selections in \texttt{Cargo.lock}. The important property for a
compiler is not that two installations happen to have the same
package names. Each importing package instance must see the same
bindings from its local dependency aliases to particular package
instances. Two lookup edges with the same label can attach to
different provider nodes.

Suppose an animation tool uses a scene reader and a thumbnail
writer. The reader is written for version 1 of a color package;
the writer is written for version 2. Our package manager allows
multiple installed versions if their component ABIs can coexist.
The package's module names alone are no longer adequate global
keys. \texttt{Color.Pixel} from version 1 does not equal
\texttt{Color.Pixel} from version 2 by spelling. A palette
converter may translate between explicit types, but silently
unifying the identities can mix layouts and assumptions.

\section{Record What Each Edge Actually Selects}

In \Cref{fig:visual-402}, both edge labels read \texttt{colors}
but lead to different instances. If the language gives manifests
the power to rename aliases, the reader can call the package
\texttt{colors} while its source registry uses a longer name.
The lock graph records the binding by importer, not just a
deduplicated list of fetched packages.

