Choose and state an identity policy. In this toy system an
instance binds one immutable source coordinate and one resolved
feature context. Targets that choose different module roots
use different build variants. Some package managers unify
features for a version within a resolution context, while others
can build variants. Features may affect which declarations or
dependencies exist, so the policy cannot be reconstructed from
the version number after compilation.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Replay the Locked Graph Without Solving Again}

\begin{verbatim}
replay(manifest, lock, context):
    require_lock_schema_supported(lock)
    root := lock.root_for(context)
    check_root_request_matches(root, manifest, context)
    work := [root]
    seen := {}
    while work not empty:
        id := work.pop()
        if id in seen: continue
        seen.add(id)
        node := lock.nodes[id]
        contents := fetch_exact(node.source, node.revision)
        require_digest(contents, node.digest)
        requests := active_requests(contents.manifest, node, context)
        require_same_aliases(requests, node.edges)
        for (alias, request) in requests:
            destination := lock.nodes[node.edges[alias]]
            require_matches(destination, request)
            work.push(destination.id)
        install_module_roots(id, contents, node.features, context)
    return resolver_environment(root, reachable_edges(lock, seen))
\end{verbatim}

Validation must check feature constraints and source policy in
addition to version ranges. The active-request function runs on
the chosen feature configuration, including any applicable
target conditions. If a locked request no longer matches, fail
with the importing node and alias instead of silently running a
new solve. A package archive with the correct name but an
unexpected digest is also a failed replay, even if its visible
interfaces happen to be identical. Sorting the lock encoding
can make reviews easier without altering semantic order.

\section{Distinguish a Dependency Upgrade from an Ambient Change}

For an intentional upgrade, seed a solve from the lock graph
and constrain unchanged edges where the policy allows. If a
requested upgrade necessitates a transitive change, explain the
chain of newly incompatible constraints. A fixture with one
allowed range and two candidates tests update behavior; a locked
build must ignore the later candidate and load the previously
pinned one. A version solver that sometimes chooses the later
version because a different registry mirror listed it first
has turned an ambient condition into a dependency input.

\begin{verbatim}
input                         replay result
same lock, newer release      retain locked node
same lock, missing archive    fail unless cached exact bytes
manifest excludes locked 1.4  reject; request new resolution
alias points to new digest    a changed input, review the lock
\end{verbatim}

Mutable workspace paths require a different promise: either
snapshot them, record a checked tree digest, or state that the
workspace source is another uncontrolled input. Likewise an
identical import graph alone does not guarantee bitwise identical
executables. Compiler and linker versions, build options,
generated files, and capabilities of build scripts can all
affect output. To debug a reproducibility failure, first compare
the resolved graph, then artifact inputs and build context.
Keeping those records distinct tells the team whether a change
came from a solver decision or from a later build stage.

\section{Explain Failure at the Edge That Causes It}

Change the example to a package with an exclusive native
component: the package manager permits only one selected color
version in this build. The reader allows versions 1.3 and 1.4;
the writer requires 2.1. The conflict is not solved by
selecting the newest version and letting the type checker
complain. The empty intersection belongs to dependency
resolution, before the front end can construct any valid root
environments. Show the conflicting requests as paths from the
root, together with the exclusive-component constraint. If the
constraint is relaxed only for a different target, name that
target difference instead of claiming every build fails.

\begin{verbatim}
tool -> reader -> colors: allowed {1.3, 1.4}
tool -> writer -> colors: allowed {2.1}
target native-backend: one provider for color-library ABI
result: no valid simultaneous selection
\end{verbatim}

In the multiple-version case, by contrast, report item-level
type errors with both concrete instances. An argument mismatch
between two types simply printed as \texttt{Color.Pixel} is
misleading. Add package source and selected version to the
diagnostic; use dependency paths if the two labels still look
identical. The goal is to let the programmer find the
dependency edge to change, not to make the type checker behave
as a second version solver.

A resolver regression can compile a pair of fixtures using
identical source text for both color providers. The fixture
that imports the same node through two aliases sees one type;
the fixture that imports the two distinct nodes sees two.
Then rerun replay with exactly the same lock and reorder the
registry results. The graph, import identity, and diagnostics
must not change.

Run the same graph fixture under a context that disables the
writer. The writer-only package must not become an available
source import merely because its archive remains in the cache.
An offline cache is a way to satisfy exact fetches; it is not
an additional search path. The compiler should receive only
the active importer-relative environment so such a path fails
in both clean and previously populated workspaces.
